WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.162
Tell Robo to draw a shape, and
Robo draws any shape at all.

2
00:00:08.382 --> 00:00:11.725
Say a big square in the middle,
and you get exactly that.

3
00:00:12.225 --> 00:00:17.705
Robo can draw. But Robo does exactly
what it is told, and nothing more.

4
00:00:17.925 --> 00:00:23.219
You say: draw a shape. Robo draws
a squiggle, and that is a shape.

5
00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:28.037
A shape is any shape, anywhere.
Robo did exactly what you said.

6
00:00:28.257 --> 00:00:32.065
Try again. Say: draw a
big square in the middle.

7
00:00:32.285 --> 00:00:35.814
That one is precise. It says
what, how big, and where.

8
00:00:36.034 --> 00:00:41.236
So an algorithm is a precise set of
instructions, with nothing left to guess.

9
00:00:41.736 --> 00:00:46.147
A precise instruction says what
to draw, how big, and where.

10
00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:49.943
Draw a big square in the
middle. Robo draws the walls.

11
00:00:50.163 --> 00:00:54.343
Say draw a roof somewhere, and Robo
puts the roof anywhere it likes.

12
00:00:54.563 --> 00:00:59.207
Say a triangle on top of the square,
and the roof goes where a roof belongs.

13
00:00:59.427 --> 00:01:03.514
A small door at the bottom. A
window on the wall, at the top left.

14
00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:09.167
The sun in the top right of the sky.
Nothing is left for Robo to guess.

15
00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:13.940
A cup of tea has five steps.
Making it is an algorithm too.

16
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.828
Step one: get a cup. Step
two: put a tea bag in the cup.

17
00:01:18.048 --> 00:01:22.274
Step three: pour in the hot
water. The tea goes brown.

18
00:01:22.494 --> 00:01:27.324
Step four: add a little milk.
Step five: stir it with a spoon.

19
00:01:27.544 --> 00:01:30.563
Pour the water first and you
get a puddle on the table.

20
00:01:30.783 --> 00:01:36.820
Follow it exactly as it is written, one step
after another. That is a linear algorithm.

21
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.010
Before you write an algorithm, work
out which steps the task needs.

22
00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:46.363
Taking a photo needs the tablet
switched on, and the camera open.

23
00:01:46.583 --> 00:01:50.299
Point it at the cat. Tap the
round button. Look at your photo.

24
00:01:50.519 --> 00:01:54.095
It does not need a coat, a
song, or a watered plant.

25
00:01:54.315 --> 00:01:58.169
A step the task does not need
is a step that does not belong.

26
00:01:58.389 --> 00:02:02.615
Put the needed steps in order,
and you have written an algorithm.

27
00:02:03.115 --> 00:02:07.945
The same rule builds a tower. Robo
puts the red brick down first.

28
00:02:08.165 --> 00:02:14.063
Add a brick is vague: any brick, anywhere.
Robo could give you any of these.

29
00:02:14.283 --> 00:02:19.159
Put the blue brick on top of the
red brick. Now only one tower fits.

30
00:02:19.379 --> 00:02:23.605
Precise steps, in the right order,
and nothing that does not belong.

31
00:02:24.105 --> 00:02:30.096
Precise means exact, with nothing left
to guess. Vague leaves things to guess.

32
00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:34.263
An algorithm is a precise set
of instructions to do a job.

33
00:02:34.483 --> 00:02:38.802
A linear algorithm is a straight
line of steps. Follow every one.

34
00:02:39.022 --> 00:02:42.923
Work out which steps the task
needs, and leave the others out.
